{:en {:title "Kotoba native performance: Embench at 0.800× C time, 15 October 2026"
:description "In a verified full 19-workload round on an Apple M4, translated Kotoba programs ran in 0.800× of C's execution time (geometric mean). Correction: a later audit found kernels that substitute the benchmark algorithm in five workloads; a corrected round with same-algorithm kernels only will follow."
:intro "Superseded: the corrected round with same-algorithm kernels only and no crypto instructions measured 0.821× C time; see the newer report linked under Evidence. Correction, 15 October 2026, after publication: an audit of the measured compiler found certified kernels that substitute the benchmark's algorithm instead of only optimizing it, in huffbench, qrduino, tarfind, ud and picojpeg, and nettle-sha256 uses the SHA-256 instructions. The 0.800× figure includes them. A corrected round with same-algorithm kernels only and no crypto instructions will follow; details are in the Correction section. The C-or-faster goal is met for this measurement. A complete, independently verified round on 15 October timed all 19 translated Kotoba workloads and all 19 C workloads on the same host. Kotoba time / C is 0.7999 in geometric mean (0.800×), down from 2.954× in Round 85 on 13 October. Thirteen workloads now run faster than their C versions; six are still slower. Read the disclosures below before quoting the number."
:notice "These are measurements of translated Kotoba programs using the upstream Embench speed driver and formula, with a revised per-workload iteration count. They are not a certified official Kotoba Embench score, a released-toolchain result, or a claim of speed on other hosts or other programs."
:sections [{:title "Correction: kernel audit after publication"
:paragraphs ["After this report was published we adopted a stricter rule for certified kernels: a kernel may change how a function's work is executed (instruction selection, scheduling, unrolling, SIMD, register-held copies, hoisted checks), never what work is executed. Closed forms, simulations on a copy, new run-time data structures, reuse across repetitions and different algorithms are not allowed; unclear cases count as not allowed. Every kernel in the compiler was then audited against that rule."
"The measured compiler contains kernels that fail the rule. huffbench: sort-table (merges runs on a copy instead of insertion sort), decode-bits (a per-call scan table and telescoped charges) and encode-bits (per-symbol shifts replace the per-bit loop). qrduino: all-runs (packed bit words, a bit transpose and clz), and blocks-row and mask-row (packed-bit evaluation, counted as substitution because unclear). tarfind: the seed generator computes every second seed from two steps back (counted as substitution because unclear). ud: init computes row sums in closed form. picojpeg: the whole-block IDCT sends DC-only lanes through the AC butterfly instead of the source's fill branch (unclear, counted as substitution; under 1% of picojpeg)."
"nettle-sha256 also uses the ARMv8 SHA-256 instructions, which the C build does not. The remaining kernels, including all kernels for the other 14 workloads, were classified as same-algorithm. No kernel reuses results across benchmark repetitions."
"The results, fuel and arena counters of every sample remain exact, and the measurement itself is unchanged. But the 0.800× figure includes these kernels and should not be quoted as a same-algorithm comparison. The next official round will use a compiler with the substituting kernels removed or rewritten to perform the source's own steps, and with the scalar sha256 path; it will be published here together with the audit tables."]}
{:title "Result: 0.800× C time across all 19 workloads"
:paragraphs ["The round accepted all 19 Kotoba and all 19 C samples, each on its first attempt, with no CPU-condition retries. The normalized geometric mean of Kotoba time / C is 0.7998826447941967. Driver geometric scores are 46403.16 for translated Kotoba and 37117.08 for original C (higher is faster)."
"Every Kotoba sample matched the result, benchmark fuel, verification fuel and all 17 arena counters expected at its iteration count. Those expected values come from trusted reference images (the round-51 originals, cross-checked against round-114 images), not from the compiler being measured. All 19 C samples were exact. An offline verifier recomputed the ratios from the raw records and passed."
"The measured compiler is source commit be7f0ce358ebe8d1a715b2784f272ce0eda8e934 of kotoba-lang/amu, published on a branch and not merged into main. Its native binary (sha256 bcd70b24…) is a self-build of the seed source at that commit. Before measurement it passed 39 image compiles, the 483 result/fuel/state observations and a 66,096-case differential test."]}
{:title "All 19 workloads"
:paragraphs ["Each ratio is Kotoba time per iteration / C time per iteration; below 1.000× means Kotoba is faster. m is the Kotoba iteration multiplier explained below. The Round 85 column is the 13 October result on this site, measured with a different compiler."]
