{:title "Disclosures: what the number depends on"
:paragraphs ["Much of the gain comes from certified kernels. When a function's intermediate code exactly matches a recognized shape, the compiler emits hand-written AArch64 for it, with fuel accounting identical to the general path and a fallback to that path. Admission is decided by code shape, never by workload name, but the shapes were written for hot functions of these ports. Those speedups do not carry over to other programs automatically. The general SSA code generator also improved over this period, but the five workloads below 0.6× all rely on kernels."
"nettle-sha256 uses the ARMv8 SHA-256 instructions; the C build (-O2, no intrinsics) does not. Using a scalar sha256 path instead, at its local estimate of about 1.27×, would put the geometric mean at about 0.848×, still below 1.000×."
"This is one measurement cycle on one host with one accepted sample per workload. No confidence interval is claimed, and other hosts may differ: xgboost and statemate measured clearly slower relative to C on this host than in local estimates."]}
{:title "Protocol change: per-workload iteration multipliers"
:paragraphs ["The previous attempt on 15 October (an earlier compiler) aborted: Kotoba's matmult-int finished its fixed iteration count in 0.120 s, under the protocol's 0.5 s short-interval floor. Kotoba had become too fast for the earlier iteration count. The revised protocol runs each Kotoba workload LSF × 8192 × m iterations, where m is 1, 2, 4, 8 or 16, and divides the time by m. The per-workload ratio is (4 / m) × Kotoba ns / C ns, because C runs LSF × 32768 iterations."
"m was fixed before the files were transferred, from local data only, and recorded in a hashed pre-registration (sha256 d0efbc4a…). It is the smallest power of two whose projected interval reaches 1.0 s, as long as total fuel stays at or below 0.9 × 2^35. The projection divides the candidate image's instruction count by the fastest instructions per second seen for that workload in the two previous rounds. Fuel limited matmult-int (m = 8) and statemate (m = 1). An earlier pre-registration that used a broader calibration set was superseded before any timing and is kept on record."
"Unchanged: the C arm and its frozen binaries, the 0.5 s floor, the 2^35 fuel budget, the trusted loader, one warmup and one accepted sample, at most three CPU-only attempts, and taking the first qualified sample rather than the fastest. The only resource cap raised was qrduino's pair cap (65536 to 131072). Every interval cleared the floor; the shortest was matmult-int at 0.777 s. The projection is not a lower bound: actual intervals ran 0.67 to 1.76 times the projection."]}
{:title "How to verify"
:paragraphs ["The reproduction package in kotoba-lang/amu (bench/embench/reproduce-20261015) checks the result at four levels, each with one command. (a) Offline: recompute every ratio and the geometric mean from the published raw records and check all hashes; this needs no Apple hardware. (b) Build: starting from amu's public bootstrap compiler, one documented step reaches the measured compiler; its self-builds G1 = G2 = G3 reproduce the measured binary bcd70b24 byte for byte, and it recompiles all 19 ports to byte-identical images. (c) Exactness: run every image at its iteration count and compare result, fuel and the 17 arena counters with values re-derived from the trusted reference images, which are included. (d) Timing: a same-host Kotoba and C campaign with the same admission rules, which gives a host-specific ratio."
"Levels (a) to (c) were run from fresh clones and passed. C binaries depend on the clang version, so level (d) builds fresh C with the verifier's clang; timings differ by host and load."]}
{:title "Measurement method"
:paragraphs ["Both arms ran on the same Apple M4 Mac mini in the same cycle, Kotoba first and then fresh C. The C arm uses Apple clang 17.0.0 with -O2 -std=gnu11 and unchanged binaries frozen in round 65. Upstream Embench revision 09c2ed8c3b7008c95d08b038de4a3f6dc103ed70 supplies the untouched speed driver and score formula."
"CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW brackets the workload body. One warmup body, resets and verification run outside that interval; representation allocation inside the body stays timed. Admission requires at least 70% raw CPU idle before and after each sample and at least 70% background idle. The host's load gate read 90.4% median and 86.6% minimum idle before the round."]}
{:title "What is still slower than C"
:paragraphs ["xgboost (1.957×), slre (1.881×), sglib-combined (1.612×), picojpeg (1.600×), statemate (1.355×) and md5sum (1.032×) are slower than C, and wikisort is close at 0.965×. Work on picojpeg, slre, sglib, xgboost and md5sum is continuing; it is not part of this result."]}]
